<ClusterPage cluster="cluster-1">

  <!-- Hero text under the page title -->
  <Fragment slot="lede">Communities in the Lake Tanganyika Basin are increasingly affected by flooding, landslides, and environmental degradation caused by climate change, resulting in natural resource scarcity, damages to industrial facilities and public infrastructure and chronic displacement that have a profound impact on people’s livelihoods.</Fragment>

  <!-- Regional context: shown in full -->
  <Fragment slot="context">
    <p>Communities in the Lake Tanganyika Basin are increasingly affected by flooding, landslides, and environmental degradation caused by climate change, resulting in natural resource scarcity, damages to industrial facilities and public infrastructure and chronic displacement that have a profound impact on people’s livelihoods. Climate projections in the region give cause for concern, as they show that disasters will only increase in terms of their severity and frequency, meanwhile duty-bearers already struggle to cope with the negative impacts of climate change today, let alone prepare for future scenarios.</p>
    <p>Over 10 million people live in the Lake Tanganyika Basin. Most of them rely on fisheries, small scale trade and subsistence agriculture for food security and livelihoods, and are, therefore, particularly vulnerable to the impacts of climate change. In 2024, the water level of Lake Tanganyika has already risen to 776.72 meters above sea level compared to the historical average of 772.7 meters, and regional climate projections caution against increasingly severe and frequent climate-induced disasters. This situation is further exacerbated through unsustainable land management and deforestation, which increases soil erosion, sedimentation, and biodiversity loss. Struggling to adapt to increased resource scarcity in the basin, many people are left without a stable income. Populations living in on the lake shore are exposed to repeated displacement.</p>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- More context: shown behind "Read the full cluster context" -->
  <Fragment slot="context-more">
    <p>According to IOM’s Displacement Tracking Matrix (DTM) in the DRC, more than 20,000 individuals have been displaced because of the rising water of Lake Tanganyika in May 2024 only. Already living under the pressure of environmental degradation, climate change further increases the vulnerability of about 120 million people across the Congo Basin, who depend on rain-fed agriculture and basin resources for their livelihoods. Furthermore, climate change exacerbates power dynamics and structures that serve to reinforce social and gendered inequalities. Specifically, women, children, the elderly, and disabled, are disproportionately affected by the impacts of climate change due to unequal access to livelihood services, productive assets, restrictive cultural norms and limited involvement in communal decision-making processes.</p>
    <p>Since the turn of the 21st century, countries in the region like the DRC have been frequently portrayed in the international media as “the worst place in the world to be a woman”. Yet the moral and political economy of gender relations across the region has nevertheless been shaped by a long, multitudinous history of women’s experiences of agency and disempowerment, competition and solidarity, whose complexity cannot be captured through victimizing narratives. Despite limited room for maneuvering, women across the region never restricted themselves to the roles imposed on them, with several national and regional examples of emancipation and paths of mobilization. Throughout its implementation, RE2CLID will look at traditional culture and ways to support gender-transformative access to, and distribution of, natural resources and livelihoods and address the power relationships between women and others in the communities where it will intervene.</p>
    <p>Seeking alternative means to sustain themselves, hundreds of thousands of people across the region have already migrated to urban areas or joined groups associated with armed conflict or illegal mining. DTM figures show a similarly alarming trend in Burundi, with nearly 90% of the country’s internal displacement due to climate-related disasters, including floods, torrential rains, landslides, storms and hail.</p>
    <p>Lake bordering regions of the United Republic of Tanzania have hosted Burundian and DRC refugees and economic migrants for years. Kigoma region, in particular, has been hosting young migrants, agro-pastoralist and nomadic groups for decades. It is also increasingly welcoming people who migrate as a result of a combination of economic, climatic, and demographic factors from other regions in the country as well as from the Kigoma region itself. At the same time, the communities themselves experience internal displacement as soil loss, flooding, erosion, and droughts adversely affect agricultural production, livelihoods, and well-being. While these mobility patterns are centuries old and can play an important adaptation role for populations responding to environmental degradation and climate change, tensions between pastoralists and farmers are increasing as grazing lands are becoming degraded and water is becoming scarcer with progressive climate change. Meanwhile, land-use change and urbanization further restrict the amount of space available for traditional livelihoods.</p>
    <p>In general, the negative impacts of climate change in the region are compounded by existing inequalities, high dependency on agriculture and ecosystems services, unsustainable land-use management and spatial planning, as well as local conflicts. Based on a DTM assessment, approximately 96% of IDPs in Eastern DRC, including South Kivu, Tanganyika, North Kivu, and Ituri provinces are displaced due to armed groups and intercommunal conflict. These are frequently related to disputes over natural resource ownership and management, including land, forest, minerals, and water. These vital resources, in turn, are intensely exploited due to the increased needs of local communities and the influx of displaced populations from conflict areas to host communities. In this regard, climate change acts as a risk driver, exacerbating fragilities in existing socio-economic and political dynamics.</p>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- Disaster Displacement Indicators (DDI) section -->
  <Fragment slot="ddi">
    <p>The Disaster Displacement Indicators, developed by IOM and IDMC, give countries a standard way to collect, report and analyse disaster displacement, so that it can be built into DRR policy and development planning and compared across borders.</p>
    <p>In the Lake Tanganyika Basin, RE2CLID will <strong>pilot the indicators in at least two Cluster 1 countries</strong>. Countries will be selected based on needs identified with national authorities and regional actors, including the Lake Tanganyika Authority, EAC and IGAD/ICPAC. This builds on the pilot already run in Mozambique.</p>
    <p>Results will be shared through regional dialogues and will feed the UNDRR–WMO–UNDP Disaster Tracking System and loss-and-damage processes. They will also guide RE2CLID's resilience work under Specific Objective 3.</p>
    <Callout type="pending" compact title="Pilot countries to be confirmed">
      <p>The Cluster 1 pilot countries will be confirmed after the inception-phase assessment.</p>
    </Callout>
  </Fragment>

